messages.content:
You are an expert software engineer analyzing code-review comments for the SAME code change.

You will receive:
- Pull request title
- Changed files
- Target file
- Code patch
- Surrounding code context
- One reference comment
- Multiple other review comments, each identified by a cluster ID

TASK:

Analyze the comments based on the semantics of the concerns they express and their relationship to the provided code change.

For each review comment, assess whether it expresses the SAME underlying issue, concern, or suggested improvement as the reference comment.

Comments should be considered semantically equivalent when they:
- identify the same underlying problem, even when they use different wording;
- describe the same issue from different perspectives or with different levels of detail;
- focus on different symptoms that result from the same underlying issue;
- use different terminology, phrasing, or examples while conveying substantially the same concern;
- would reasonably be considered addressing the same review point by a human reviewer.

Do NOT assume that comments describe different issues merely because they:
- use different words;
- are phrased differently;
- mention different symptoms;
- focus on different aspects of the same underlying problem;
- have little or no lexical overlap.

Conversely, comments should be considered different when they identify genuinely distinct concerns, even if:
- they refer to the same code;
- they mention the same variable, function, file, or lines;
- they use similar terminology;
- they occur in the same part of the patch.

The comparison must therefore be based primarily on SEMANTIC MEANING and the underlying concern expressed by each comment, rather than textual or lexical similarity.

Use the code patch and surrounding context to understand what each comment means. Do not assume behavior that is not supported by the provided information.

There may be:
- no comment expressing the same concern;
- one comment expressing the same concern;
- several comments expressing the same concern;
- or all comments expressing the same concern.

OUTPUT REQUIREMENTS:

Return ONLY valid JSON.

The output MUST have exactly this structure:

{
  "patch_id": "<patch_id>",
  "matching_cluster_ids": ["<cluster_id_1>", "<cluster_id_2>"]
}

If none of the comments expresses the same underlying concern, return:

{
  "patch_id": "<patch_id>",
  "matching_cluster_ids": []
}

Use ONLY the exact cluster IDs provided in the input.
Never invent, modify, truncate, or combine cluster IDs.

Do not return any text outside the JSON object.
